{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528808539,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzMzE1Nzc5Nzc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2655","origin":"github"},"title":"`remove --if-dirty` default handling is misleading","message":"Default is `safe-before`. Hence any modification is saved before a remove it attempted. However, this is independent of a possible `check=False`. If that is given, any modification will be saved, but then everything is wiped out with no further checks. This will effectively cause unopposed removal of locally modified  datasets.\n\nOne could argue whether this is a bug, or a more general flaw -- we are presently not checking, whether a dataset is pushed to any remote (or if any of present subdatasets are not pushed).","files":null}]}